index 9ebd24318ce9e69802e06fa5ac0be97b1c71e5e9..c23fa3ceda7fd6ffef3e248fe54cd8a01e77b232 100644
--- a/docs/file-formats.md
+++ b/docs/file-formats.md
@@ -1159,3 +1159,45 @@ establish a timestamp. If the clock is unset, sync network time first and then
 move to the desired position. Equal timestamps leave both sides unchanged.
 Older firmware cannot read version 2; retain the firmware and progress backup
 before downgrading. TXT/XTC progress formats are unchanged.
+
+## Library view cache v2
+
+Disposable files `/.crosspoint/library-filter.cache` and
+`/.crosspoint/library-recents.cache` use a packed 30-byte little-endian header:
+`magic:u32=0x3156434c`, `version:u16=2`, `payloadBytes:u16`, `key:u64`,
+`sourceCount:u16`, `visibleCount:u16`, `processedCount:u16`, `checksum:u64`.
+The checksum combines FNV-1a of the payload with the key, source/visible/processed
+counts and payload size. Partial snapshots resume from processedCount; full ones
+restore without a filter pass.
+The full-library payload is the existing filter bitmap (at most 4096 bytes),
+with sparse ranks reconstructed in RAM. The recent payload is the fixed
+18-entry u16 history-row array; only visibleCount entries are used. Wrong keys,
+counts, versions, lengths, truncation or checksum mismatch cause a cache miss.
+Writes use a temporary file and rename; interruption safely falls back to refiltering.
+This does not change the EPUB, stats or Library index binary format.
+
+
+## Opened-book ordering (OPB1, version 1)
+
+Home cover browsing uses `/.crosspoint/opened.idx`, newest opened first, plus
+`opened.paths`. No book contents, titles, authors or cover pixels are duplicated
+in these files. The normal library cover cache remains the source of thumbnails.
+
+The little-endian packed index header is 20 bytes: magic u32 `0x3142504f`,
+version u16 (1), entry stride u16 (20), entry count u32, revision u64. Each
+20-byte entry stores complete-path FNV-1a hash u64, path payload offset u32 and
+last-opening order u64. Opening order is a persistent monotonic counter, so
+sorting does not depend on the device clock being set. The newest-first index
+supports direct seeks to a page; opening Home does not walk the whole history.
+
+The append-only path file stores u16 byte length followed by UTF-8 path bytes
+(1–511 bytes). Paths are hash-checked when read. Reopening reuses the existing
+path payload and moves its small index entry to the front. Metadata refreshes
+of the current book do not rewrite the ordering. Rename keeps its position;
+removal drops only that entry. No fixed history-book count is imposed.
+
+Updates stream the small index through `opened.tmp`, sync it, rotate the old
+index to `opened.bak`, then publish. The old snapshot can be recovered if a
+write/rename is interrupted. An unpublished path tail is harmless. Legacy recent
+entries seed one complete snapshot, preserving order; later history comes from
+actual opens. History already evicted by older firmware cannot be recovered.
